# Tiny Redis – Project Roadmap

## Objective

Build a simplified version of Redis to understand:

* Command parsing
* Hash table (dictionary) data structures
* O(1) lookup operations
* Database engine design
* Persistence
* Software architecture

---

# Phase 1 – Storage Engine

The Storage Engine is the **heart of Redis**.

Internally, the database stores data as a Python dictionary.

```python
storage = {}
```

### Supported Commands

```text
SET name Batman
GET name
DEL name
EXISTS name
KEYS
FLUSHALL
```

### Example

User enters:

```text
SET age 22
```

Stored internally:

```python
storage["age"] = "22"
```

Another example:

```text
SET website thaagam.org
SET counter 10
```

Internal structure:

```python
{
    "age": "22",
    "website": "thaagam.org",
    "counter": "10"
}
```

---

## Time Complexity

| Operation | Complexity |
| --------- | ---------- |
| SET       | O(1)       |
| GET       | O(1)       |
| DEL       | O(1)       |
| EXISTS    | O(1)       |
| KEYS      | O(n)       |
| FLUSHALL  | O(n)       |

---

# Phase 2 – Command Parser

The parser converts raw user input into commands that the storage engine understands.

### User Input

```text
SET age 22
```

Python receives:

```python
command = input()
```

Parser output:

```python
["SET", "age", "22"]
```

---

## Input Cases to Handle

The parser should correctly handle or reject different formats.

Valid examples:

```text
SET age 22
SET age -22
SET age +22
SET website thaagam.org
SET user:1 Batman
SET counter 100
```

Edge cases:

```text
SET [age] 22
SET -age +22
SET name premraj sivakumar
SET name premraj-sivakumar
SET premraj-sivakumar name
SET premraj sivakumar name
name SET premraj
```

Think about which inputs should be accepted and which should return an error.

---

# Storage Engine Responsibilities

The Storage Engine should expose methods such as:

```python
add_data()
get_data()
delete_data()
edit_data()
is_key_exist()
list_keys()
flushall()
save_all()
```

---

# Example Database

Commands

```text
SET user:1 {...}
SET user:2 {...}
SET session:abc123 {...}
SET counter 10
SET website thaagam.org
```

Internal storage

```python
{
    "user:1": {...},
    "user:2": {...},
    "session:abc123": {...},
    "counter": "10",
    "website": "thaagam.org"
}
```

Notice that Redis stores everything using a **Key → Value** structure.

